{
  "id": "zustandspassiv",
  "name": "Present perfect continuous",
  "level": "B1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "Use have been + -ing for something that started in the past and is still going on: I've been waiting for an hour. Use it too when the action has only just stopped and you can see the result: You're wet. Have you been running?",
  "briefMarks": [
    "have been + -ing",
    "still going on",
    "just stopped"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-b1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "have / has + been",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "verb + -ing",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "started before · still going on · or just stopped"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "I<span class=\"vp-verb\">'ve been</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">waiting</span> for an hour. · She<span class=\"vp-verb\">'s been</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">learning</span> Spanish since March.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>have / has + been + verb-ing</strong>. <em>I've been working. She's been working.</em> Negative: <em>haven't / hasn't been working</em>. Question: <em>Have you been working? Has she been working?</em>",
        "tag": "Form"
      },
      {
        "html": "Use it for an action that <strong>started in the past and is still going on</strong>. It often answers the question <em>How long?</em> and goes with <em>for, since, all day, lately</em>: <em>I've been living here for three years. How long have you been waiting?</em>",
        "tag": "Use 1: still going on"
      },
      {
        "html": "Use it for an action that has <strong>just stopped</strong> when you can see or feel the <strong>result</strong>: <em>Your hands are dirty! — I've been cleaning the garage. You look tired. Have you been working late?</em>",
        "tag": "Use 2: just stopped"
      },
      {
        "html": "The continuous is about the <strong>activity</strong> (how long, not finished): <em>I've been reading all day.</em> The present perfect simple is about the <strong>result</strong> (how many, finished): <em>I've read three chapters.</em>",
        "tag": "Activity or result?"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>State verbs</strong> do not take <em>-ing</em>: <em>know, like, love, want, need, belong, have</em> (= own). Use the present perfect simple: <em>I've known Priya for years. He's had that car since 2018.</em>",
        "tag": "State verbs"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>I'm waiting for an hour.</em> → ✓ <em>I've been waiting for an hour.</em> (it started in the past) · ✗ <em>I've been wait here.</em> → ✓ <em>I've been waiting here.</em> (always -ing after been) · ✗ <em>I've been knowing Tom for years.</em> → ✓ <em>I've known Tom for years.</em> (know is a state verb)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "For the present perfect simple, see <a href=\"/present-perfect\">Present perfect vs past simple</a>. For the difference between <em>for</em> and <em>since</em>, see <a href=\"/for-since-ago\">for, since, ago</a>. The <em>-ing</em> form is the same as in the <a href=\"/present-continuous\">present continuous</a>. With <em>live, work</em> and <em>study</em> both forms mean almost the same: <em>I've lived / I've been living here for years.</em> Do not use the continuous when you say <em>how many</em>: <em>I've written five emails</em>, not <em>I've been writing five emails</em>.",
